.TH IAD\-REORDERABLE\-LIST 3 "" "iced-adaptive-design 0.1.0" "iced-adaptive-design Manual"
.SH NAME
reorderable_list \- See the module
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
ReorderableList::new(items: &'a [T], row: impl Fn(&T) \-> Element<'a, M> + 'a)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
See the module.
.PP
\fBReorderableList\fR: a list that you declare once with its two views, \fBpreview\fR and \fBedit\fR, and that owns the switching between them; editing means dragging the rows into a new order (libadwaita's draggable rows: a grip, \fBlist\-drag\-handle\-symbolic\fR, at the end of each). Declared the way Chakra's \fBEditable\fR is (a preview, an edit view and their controls in one container), but controlled, like every component here: the order and the mode live in a \fBReorderState\fR, and the list speaks \fBone\fR message, \fBReorderEvent\fR.
.PP
.nf
.RS
// view
ReorderableList::of(&self.order, |item| action_row(*item).into())
    .title("Sidebar")
    .on_event(Msg::List)

// update
Msg::List(event) => { self.order.apply(event); }
.RE
.fi
.PP
That is the whole wiring: \fBof\fR takes the items and the mode from the state, the Edit / Cancel / Done buttons and the drag all come out as \fBon_event\fR, and \fBapply\fR handles every one (the draft, submit, cancel). The state can sit anywhere in the model.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBViews.\fR One row builder serves both; \fBedit_row\fR gives the edit view its own (a compact row, say). The edit view adds the grips. Only the view being shown is built.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBControls.\fR \fBcontrols(false)\fR drops the bar when the app has its own Edit button (send it \fBReorderEvent::Edit\fR); \fBdisabled\fR dims Edit; \fBlabels\fR renames the buttons.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSwitching.\fR The views are picked like the variants of a \fBMetaComponent\fR keyed by \fBMode\fR (\fBTrigger::selected(Mode::Edit)\fR, the same \fBprimitives::meta::pick\fR, called directly because \fBresponsive\fR cannot shrink to its content's height; \fBForm\fR does the same). A \fBMetaParentComponent\fR can switch a list as well: register it with \fBactions\fR and call \fBediting\fR with the mode it is given.
.PP
\fBDragging.\fR Press a row's grip and move: the row lifts and follows the pointer, the others move out of its way, and releasing drops it in the slot it is over (\fBReorder { from, to }\fR). Escape puts it back. Rows keep their own pointer behavior (buttons, entries) in edit mode; only the grip starts a drag. Moving rows with the keyboard is not included: iced has no focus model to hang it on yet.
.SH CONSTRUCTORS
.TP
.B ReorderableList::new(items: &'a [T], row: impl Fn(&T) \-> Element<'a, M> + 'a)
\fBitems\fR in their current order and the closure that builds one row (an \fBActionRow\fR, for instance, without suffix: the grip is added).
.TP
.B ReorderableList::of(state: &'a ReorderState<T>, row: impl Fn(&T) \-> Element<'a, M> + 'a)
A list over a \fBReorderState\fR: its items, and edit mode while it has a draft. The usual way to build one.
.TP
.B ReorderableList::actions()
The actions this list announces when it registers with a \fBMetaParentComponent\fR: the two modes it can be switched to.
.TP
.B ReorderableList::pick(editing: bool)
The view a list shows: the first variant whose trigger fires, \fBPreview\fR being the fallback.
.SH PROPERTIES
Builder methods, chained after the constructor.
.TP
.B \&.title(title: impl Into<String>)
A heading at the start of the controls bar.
.TP
.B \&.editing(editing: bool)
Whether the edit view shows (controlled; Chakra's \fBisEditing\fR).
.TP
.B \&.disabled(disabled: bool)
A disabled list cannot be put into edit mode (the Edit button is dimmed).
.TP
.B \&.controls(show: bool)
Whether the bar with the Edit / Cancel / Done buttons shows (default true). Without it the app drives \fBediting\fR from its own buttons.
.TP
.B \&.lift(scale: f32)
How much bigger the held row grows while it is dragged, as a scale (default 1.02, a tiny hover; 1.0 turns it off). The row is always opaque and drawn above the others.
.TP
.B \&.labels(edit: impl Into<String>, submit: impl Into<String>, cancel: impl Into<String>)
The labels of the three buttons (defaults: Edit, Done, Cancel).
.TP
.B \&.metrics(m: Metrics)
.SH SLOTS
Builder methods that take children.
.TP
.B \&.edit_row(row: impl Fn(&T) \-> Element<'a, M> + 'a)
The row of the edit view, when it should differ from the preview's.
.TP
.B \&.row_action(action: impl Fn(&T) \-> Element<'a, M> + 'a)
A control at the end of every row of the edit view, \fBbefore the grip\fR: a delete button, a menu. It is built from the item like the row, and keeps its own pointer behavior (only the grip starts a drag). The list's \fBReorderState::remove\fR is what a delete button wants.
.SH EVENTS
Callbacks that produce the application's message.
.TP
.B \&.on_event(f: impl Fn(ReorderEvent) \-> M + 'a)
The list's one message: Edit, Submit and Cancel from the buttons, and \fBReorder\fR from a drop. Feed it to \fBReorderState::apply\fR.
.TP
.B \&.on_change(f: impl Fn(usize, usize) \-> Option<M> + 'a)
A second, independent callback for the same drop, for whoever watches the list from outside: a \fBMetaParentComponent\fR hands one out (\fBReporter::on_change\fR) so it can react to its child's order changing while the app's own \fBon_event\fR still edits the order. Both messages are published, \fBon_event\fR first.
